WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.540
Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.513
So first of all,
great to see you here.

00:00:09.513 --> 00:00:10.680
My name is Daniele Gammelli.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.560
I'm a postdoc in the
Autonomous Systems

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.920
Lab in the Department of
Aeronautics and Astronautics.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.500
As you might guess from the
accent, I'm also Italian.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.427
So if optimal control
doesn't work for you,

00:00:20.427 --> 00:00:22.760
I guess you'll be experts in
Italian accents at the very

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.520
least.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.000
So for today, the plan is
that-- so over the last week,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.880
we started introducing
the general formalism

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.000
for optimal control.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.440
Specifically, we introduced the
general problem formulation,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.880
which is something along
the lines of-- we're

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:48.480
given a dynamical
system x of t, which

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.360
is in the form of a
differential equation.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:59.200
And the overall goal for us was
to determine an optimal control

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:08.960
signal, u star of t, that
minimizes some objective, which

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.160
we defined as having a
terminal cost h of x of tf

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:21.220
for the final time tf
plus our running cost.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.520
And then so this we
realized that this

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.400
was in an infinite dimensional.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.110
Yes?

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.360
Can you use a different board?

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.040
All right.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.480
Again, nothing new here.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.200
What I was saying here is we
defined the optimal control

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:47.120
problem as a dynamical system,
where we wanted to define

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.800
an optimal control u star of t.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:56.680
And this control then steers the
state trajectory x of t in a way

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.160
that we can optimize
an objective function

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.080
j, which is composed of two
terms, a terminal state--

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.360
terminal cost h of x of tf
evaluated at the final time

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.920
plus a running cost.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.140
Again, I don't want to just--
don't take too much out of this

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.132
exactly, because we
saw this already.

00:02:18.132 --> 00:02:19.840
And then given that
we realized that this

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.020
is an infinite dimensional
optimization problem,

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.680
we then went into--
opened a parenthesis

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.880
about finite dimensional
optimization,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.480
where we started
by defining, first

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.400
of all, what we mean by
minima or maxima, that are

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.160
the objects of interest for us.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.600
And then we went into defining
necessary optimality conditions

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.480
for the unconstrained
case first,

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.880
namely the fact that
gradient of f of x

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.400
needs to be equal to 0,
so be a stationary point

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.280
of our objective function f.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.200
And then we did the same for
the constrained case, namely

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.280
the equality constraint case.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.400
And so for today,
we'll just quickly

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.320
wrap up last week's
lecture, where

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.240
we will be defining the
necessary optimality conditions

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.600
for the inequality
constrained case.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.640
And then s move to the
content of today's topic,

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.980
which are then essentially our
first step into optimal control,

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.720
where we'll be trying to
have a similar approach that

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.100
is defining necessary
optimality conditions,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.520
this time for the infinite
dimensional optimal control

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:29.480
problem.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.260
Any questions on the plan
and does this make sense?

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.740
All right.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:38.240
OK.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.320
So first of all, when we
say inequality constraints,

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.000
we essentially refer to
an optimization problem

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.807
of this form.

00:03:45.807 --> 00:03:47.640
So we're minimizing,
as usual, our objective

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.280
function f of x subject to some
equality constraints, which

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.580
is exactly the setting
we were seeing last week.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.080
So h of i of x needs to be equal
to 0 for m equality constraints.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.200
And then here, we're introducing
our inequality constraints

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.320
represented as gj of x.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.200
It needs to be less
than or equal than 0.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.480
And we're assuming here that
f, h, and all the gj's are

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.200
continuously differentiable.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.800
Again, this problem can be
written in compact form, which

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.720
we will be referring to as
the ICP, so the inequality

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.240
constraint problem, where we
are essentially compacting

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.860
this in vector notation.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:30.303
So all the letters become
basically boldface.

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:31.720
But this is the
kind of problem we

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.680
are looking at when we say
inequality constraint optimal

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:35.700
control problem.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.760
And so when we introduce
inequality constraints, kind

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.560
of mathematically, this object
becomes much more difficult.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.840
And we could spend
a series of lectures

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:54.840
into defining the optimality
constraints for this case.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.400
However, what I will do
today is essentially,

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.800
I will try to take an
easy route in defining

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.320
the optimality constraints.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.360
And what I mean by
easy route is that we

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.280
will try to reuse as
much as possible what

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.440
we derived last week with
the equality constraints.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.200
And the way we do this is
through something which we

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.880
refer to as active constraint.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.160
So specifically for
any feasible point,

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.960
the set of active
inequality constraints

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:28.000
is denoted as this A of
x, which is essentially

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.520
the set of all the
inequality constraints,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:36.440
gj's, that are respected
at the equality.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.920
On the other hand, all
the other constraints

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.320
that are not respected at
the equality, so are strictly

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:48.120
less than 0, we will be
defining them as inactive at x.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:55.560
So more visually,
say that we have

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.520
a problem along the
lines of minimizing

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:06.720
x plus y, subject to the fact
that x and y must be greater

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:14.823
than 0, and x plus y
needs to be less than 3.

00:06:14.823 --> 00:06:16.240
What we're looking
at is something

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.657
where-- first of
all, it has to be

00:06:17.657 --> 00:06:19.760
in the positive
side of the plane.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:26.700
And then we have
this constraint here,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.960
which essentially defines
the feasible region

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.520
to be this one over here.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.120
Can you guys see from
the back over there?

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.400
Yeah.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.480
So in this case,
the minimum-- so

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:43.000
if you look at the level curves
of our objective functions,

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.800
there are something
that look like this.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.440
And so in this case, the minimum
point would be at the origin.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:55.890
So if we take this
point, so 0, 0,

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.250
at this point out of
the three constraints,

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.090
we have that these two are
respected at the equality.

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:03.970
So these two will be active.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:06.370
On the other hand, this
constraint over here

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:07.830
is not respected
at the equality.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:08.950
So this is inactive.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.562
OK.

00:07:15.562 --> 00:07:18.210
And so given that this
definition is clear,

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:22.130
this point is where
most of, I would say,

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:26.770
the brainpower for understanding
this easy route goes into.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:30.930
Specifically what we're saying
here is that if x of star

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.770
is a local minimum
of the ICP, which

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:36.930
is the full problem that we
saw on the previous slides,

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:42.250
so this one here, what we're
saying is that then x of star

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:46.890
is also a local minimum
for the identical ICP

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:50.810
without the inactive
constraints.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:55.890
And to give you an intuition,
the proof for this argument

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:59.570
usually goes by contradiction.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.450
So we're saying
that x of star is

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:03.470
a local minimum of
the original ICP,

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.770
so with all
constraints included.

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:07.410
But let us assume
that this is not

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.890
a local minimum for
the problem without

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.890
the inactive constraints.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:16.290
So if it is not a local
minimum, what this would mean

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.890
is that we would be able to
move in the neighborhood of that

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:24.490
x of star while
respecting the constraints

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:29.970
and still finding a better
value of the objective function.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.309
At the same time, so if we are
moving into a neighborhood,

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:35.970
we can find a neighborhood
that is arbitrarily

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:39.370
small, such that the
inactive constraints always

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.890
remain inactive.

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:45.650
And if you're following me
until here, what we're saying

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:49.130
is that if this is true,
then all the constraints

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.307
of the original problem
would still be satisfied.

00:08:52.307 --> 00:08:54.890
And so this would mean that we
would have found a better point

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:56.890
also for the original problem.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:03.530
Therefore, kind of concluding
the contradiction here.

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:04.250
Right.

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:08.530
So if you will--

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:12.090
again, don't take
me by the exact word

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:15.170
here, but what we are
saying is that we can always

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:19.050
think of a neighborhood
of this point x star.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:21.410
So if this is not a
minimum of this problem,

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.650
we can define a neighborhood
that is arbitrarily small, such

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:29.970
that we can move along this
minimum and find a new point

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:35.690
x prime, such that the value
of the function at x prime

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:40.203
is smaller than the
original f of x prime.

00:09:40.203 --> 00:09:42.370
And specifically, since I
can take this neighborhood

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:45.770
to be arbitrarily small, I
could move in the direction

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.790
that it satisfies all of
my original constraints,

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:52.490
so the equality constraints, but
also the ones that are inactive,

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:54.650
because the inactive
ones always have-- you

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:57.650
could think about it intuitively
as there is some margin

00:09:57.650 --> 00:09:59.690
that I can play with.

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:01.610
And so if this is
true, then I would

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.810
have found a point that is
better for the original problem

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:05.310
as well.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:08.970
And so this is not the case.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:09.950
Does this make sense?

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:16.860
OK.

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:20.290
And so what we're seeing here
is that at the local minimum,

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:23.010
active inequality
constraints can essentially

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:26.150
be treated to a large
extent as equalities.

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.850
And so you're probably
starting to guess a little bit

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:33.130
where we're going now.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:34.930
So remember, what
we're trying to do here

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:37.570
is define the optimality
conditions for inequality

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:38.430
constraint problems.

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:41.130
And we said, we want to take
the, quote unquote, "easy route"

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:44.370
of leveraging as much as
possible the results that we got

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.170
in the equality constraint case.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:47.890
And so here what
we just proved is

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:51.350
that if x is a local
minimum of the full problem,

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:54.410
that is the full inequality
constraint problem,

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:57.810
then it is also local minimum
for the version of the problem

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:01.610
where I'm only considering
the active constraints.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:02.930
Yes?

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:05.530
When you're saying that it's
a local of the constraint,

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:07.690
are you assuming that
you're not on the boundary?

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.490
Even with that example, if
you cross the boundary out

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.970
of the constraints, you
could be going lower

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:14.570
or it would not be local.

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:15.350
Yeah, exactly.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.690
So this is what I meant by
don't take it too rigorously

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.050
here, because here, there
is actually only this one

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:23.450
point that we're considering.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:25.770
But maybe you could
imagine something

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:28.850
like in three
dimensions, maybe, where

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:34.210
I have my inequality constraints
being some kind of hyperplanes,

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:39.690
and satisfying both means lying
on this subspace over here.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:46.730
Then you could think that moving
in a neighborhood of this point,

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:52.470
I could still respect my
original equality constraints.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:53.570
Does this [INAUDIBLE]?

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:55.690
As long as you are
not on the boundary,

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:58.530
essentially, is what that
previous point was saying,

00:11:58.530 --> 00:11:59.390
then that is true.

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:01.260
Because we are on the boundary.

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.920
You can cross the boundary and
become a lower [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:06.170
No, of course, we want to
respect all the constraints.

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:06.690
Yes.

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:08.610
The previous point,
only-- you're

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:13.610
saying only is of concern if
you are not on the boundary.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.765
By previous point, you
mean that also the--

00:12:15.765 --> 00:12:17.890
The local minimum of that
is also the local minimum

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:18.890
without the constraints.

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:21.090
That's only true if the
local minimum is only

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:22.530
within the boundary and not--

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:24.710
If the local minimum
is a feasible point.

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:25.650
Yes.

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:27.610
So it's a local--
it's within the bounds

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.570
and not on the boundary.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:30.610
If it's on--

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:32.810
Well, in this case, it
is a boundary, right?

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:33.570
Yeah.

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:36.610
But if crossing the boundary
outside of the constraints

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:39.870
means you have a
lower point, then--

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:43.050
so the local minimum without
the active rates is a different

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:46.405
local minimum than with the
constraints, because you're--

00:12:46.405 --> 00:12:48.530
one is in the boundary.
one is not in the boundary.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:49.330
Yes.

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:53.630
Well, if you're going across the
boundary in the feasible region,

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:54.728
then yes.

00:12:54.728 --> 00:12:56.270
That means you don't
care about that.

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:57.090
Yeah.

00:12:57.090 --> 00:13:01.210
So then that is only true
then when you're within--

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:04.730
you're not on the
boundary, right, 0.1?

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:08.210
I mean, again, the fact is
that boundary here it depends,

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:09.130
because we--

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:10.890
boundary-- I guess,
you're saying when

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:13.050
these constraints are active.

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:15.710
So some of them will be at
the boundaries and some won't.

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:16.210
Yeah.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:17.585
So I guess I'm
confused where you

00:13:17.585 --> 00:13:19.830
say it's a local minimum
without the constraints,

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:22.150
just because you have a local
minimum within the constraints.

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:24.290
But if you're in the boundary,
that's not necessarily true.

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:26.248
Because when you talk
about the previous thing,

00:13:26.248 --> 00:13:29.830
it's like on the boundary,
you might have a more minimum,

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:31.850
you have a lower
value, but because you

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:34.950
can't cross the boundary, you
have to keep the local boundary.

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:36.450
Yeah.

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:40.410
So it's only a local minimum
outside of the constraints

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:42.350
if you are within
the boundary itself.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:46.010
Yes, I think that's about-- yes.

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.990
Does the general picture
make sense apart from this?

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:49.810
Yeah.

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:52.550
Any other questions on this?

00:13:58.090 --> 00:13:58.590
OK.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:00.048
So basically what
we're saying here

00:14:00.048 --> 00:14:02.630
is that x is a local
minimum of the original ICP,

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:05.130
then it is also a local
minimum for the same problem

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:08.210
where we remove the inactive
inequality constraints

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:11.890
and only keep the active ones.

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.510
And if we think about
what we did last week,

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:19.570
this is an object we can
basically work with and derive

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:21.530
optimality conditions.

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:35.810
So remember that last time we
were defining the Lagrangian

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:40.450
as L of x lambda, we're
introducing some Lagrangian

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:45.810
multipliers lambda, equals f
of x plus the summation from i

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:48.170
to m, which is where m
is the number of equality

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:56.330
constraints of lambda i times,
I think we call them h, so hi

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:57.170
of x.

00:14:57.170 --> 00:15:02.810
And then we use this to derive
necessary optimality conditions,

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:06.250
such that the gradient
of the Lagrangian

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:07.770
needs to be equal to 0.

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:17.610
Can you use a darker marker?

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:19.910
I think this was
the darker marker.

00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:27.150
So again, this is
from last lecture.

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:28.650
So what we're just
saying is that we

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:32.450
use this to define necessary
optimality conditions.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:33.830
So we define the Lagrangian.

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:38.610
And then we just said
that the gradient

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:42.530
with respect to this Lagrangian
function needs to be equal to 0.

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:44.210
So these were the
optimality conditions

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:46.330
in the equality
constraint case, which

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:49.170
boil down to a
system of n plus n

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:53.565
equations in n plus m unknowns.

00:15:53.565 --> 00:15:55.690
And so what I'm saying here
is that this is exactly

00:15:55.690 --> 00:16:00.090
the same case now
that we did this step.

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:02.530
And so we can apply the
exact same reasoning

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:07.250
in defining the optimality
conditions for the ICP

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:14.010
with the inactive constraints
removed, where again, this

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:17.850
is exactly the same necessary
optimality conditions we derived

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:21.330
for the equality constraint case
with the addition of this term

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:26.402
over here, which takes care of
only the active constraints,

00:16:26.402 --> 00:16:28.610
that is the inequality
constraints that are respected

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:34.170
at the boundary, where again,
we have this Lagrangian term mu

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:37.550
j star times the gradient of
the inequality constraints.

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:40.010
And everything has
to be equal to 0.

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:43.310
Or equivalently, we can
write the exact same thing.

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:46.930
So here nothing new, just
rewriting a little bit more

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:50.290
elegantly where rather than
having a summation over only

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:52.290
the active
constraints, I can have

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:57.010
a summation over all inequality
constraints, so from r1 to r,

00:16:57.010 --> 00:17:00.130
with the additional
condition that the mu's

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:06.609
