00:22:04.905 --> 00:22:05.640
All right.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:10.260
So all of this to boils down
towards the necessary optimality

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:12.700
conditions for the inequality
constraint problems, which

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:17.300
are known as Karush-Kuhn-Tucker
conditions or KKT, which

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:18.640
essentially goes as follows.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:20.780
So we, once again,
define the Lagrangian

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:24.620
function L of x lambda
mu, where lambda mu are

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:27.380
Lagrangian multipliers.

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:30.100
We used this object
then to define

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:32.060
the optimality conditions.

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:38.140
And so the theorem of basically
the KKT condition states that

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:43.340
if x of star is a local
minimum for the full inequality

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:49.260
constraint problem, where f,
h, and g's are all continuously

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:53.180
differentiable, and we assume
that x of star is [INAUDIBLE],

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:54.660
again, kind of--

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:56.480
you can ignore this for now.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:58.420
But in general, we're
saying that the equality

00:22:58.420 --> 00:22:59.980
and active inequality
constraints

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.580
are-- the gradients are
linearly independent.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:05.300
Then it means that there exists
a unique Lagrange multiplier

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:08.500
vectors, so the lambdas
and the mu's, such

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:13.220
that the gradient of the
Lagrangian is equal to 0.

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:17.420
The mu j's in correspondence
to the inactive constraints

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:18.680
are equal to 0.

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:21.268
And in general, they're all
positive, so non-negative.

00:23:21.268 --> 00:23:23.060
Again, so here, we're
just putting together

00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:25.960
the results we arrived at.

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:29.520
OK.

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:33.740
So now to ground the
intuition a little bit--

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:34.460
yes?

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:37.620
In this case, we are presently
only checking the boundaries,

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:39.620
because we're only checking
whether it is equal.

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:42.340
And so if we find one on the
boundary, then we're like,

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:43.260
oh, we found it.

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:47.440
We never checked within
the boundaries, right?

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.380
So this is a
necessary condition.

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:54.360
Meaning if a point
is a local minimum,

00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:57.260
it has to satisfy this
condition over here.

00:23:57.260 --> 00:23:59.700
So it does not mean that all--

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.860
so what it means is
that all local minimums

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:03.480
satisfy this condition.

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:03.980
Yeah.

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:07.260
So for example,
x sub 1, 1, which

00:24:07.260 --> 00:24:09.780
is none of those
constraints you have there,

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:12.740
in terms of the
equality constraints.

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:14.520
Because it's not x equals 0.

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:15.600
It's not y equals 0.

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:18.220
And it's not y plus x equals 3.

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:21.400
It would never show
up in your set.

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:24.820
The a, 1, 1 would be
nothing of the set.

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:27.460
So you would never
have it on there,

00:24:27.460 --> 00:24:29.460
but it could be a
minimum in this problem.

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:34.260
X sub 1, 1 could be a minimum?

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:37.820
Yeah, it could be because it
satisfies all the constraints.

00:24:37.820 --> 00:24:40.200
And let's say the gradient and--

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:42.020
[INAUDIBLE] the
gradient is, right?

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:45.820
But it's never being checked
in your problem, because a of 1

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:49.300
is nothing with that,
because it's not equal to 0.

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:50.220
X is not equal to 0.

00:24:50.220 --> 00:24:51.120
Y is not equal to 0.

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:54.020
And x plus y is not equal to 3.

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:57.200
I'm afraid I'm not exactly
following your argument,

00:24:57.200 --> 00:24:58.940
but I would have
to take that on you

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:00.920
because we have a
full lecture to cover.

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:03.260
I'm happy to stay afterwards
if you want offline.

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:06.940
Yes?

00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:09.740
Isn't it necessary
condition that at least one

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:13.740
of the inequality
constraints is active?

00:25:13.740 --> 00:25:17.060
The necessary condition on what?

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:18.260
To solve the point.

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:22.700
To have it be a solution
for the problems.

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:28.860
There are at least-- one of
the constraints is inner.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:32.260
So it's a property of problems
that usually solutions

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:35.500
are at tension points
between objective function

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:36.200
and constraints.

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:39.300
And so usually, yes.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:39.840
All right.

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:47.060
So to make a small example of
what it means to apply this,

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:51.700
hopefully to ground
the intuition,

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:53.100
so let us solve
this problem over

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:58.500
here where we want
to minimize x squared

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:03.360
plus epsilon squared subject
to one inequality constraints.

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:07.740
That is 2x plus y less
than or equal than 2.

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:09.460
Can you see with this marker?

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:10.320
Is it good enough?

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:10.820
Yes.

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:12.420
Yeah.

00:26:12.420 --> 00:26:13.040
All right.

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:15.520
And let's be methodical, right?

00:26:15.520 --> 00:26:20.020
So let's apply the
KKT conditions.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:25.500
So first of all, what we need is
we need to compute the gradient

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:33.760
of f, which in this case
is simply 2x and 2y, right?

00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:37.820
And the gradient of our
inequality constraints,

00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:43.500
so grad of g, which
is 2 and 1, right?

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:46.220
So we're using this
to characterize

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:52.020
the gradient of our Lagrangian.

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:54.860
And the inequality constraints
are slightly trickier

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:57.240
in the sense that you
need to consider cases,

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.580
in the case of the constraints
are active or inactive.

00:27:00.580 --> 00:27:04.260
And so let's look at
the first case, case 1,

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:07.500
where we are assuming that
the constraint is active.

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:12.760
That is that 2x plus
y is equal to 2.

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:22.260
Now, we indeed write that the
gradient of the Lagrangian is

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:29.580
equal to 0 condition, which
essentially means that 2xy plus

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:37.360
mu times our gradient of our
constraints is equal to 0.

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:43.740
So this is our gradient of the
Lagrangian has to be equal to 0.

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:46.540
And of course, we also have that
the mu's need to be positive.

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:47.720
Mu needs to be positive.

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:51.060
Well, non-negative.

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:58.360
From this, we can write
that 2x plus 2 mu equals 0,

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:04.300
2y plus mu equals 0, which
essentially means that x is

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:10.560
equal to negative mu and y is
equal to negative mu over 2.

00:28:14.700 --> 00:28:17.900
OK.

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:20.140
All right.

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:25.720
Now, if we plug these into
our active constraint,

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:30.820
so we have values for
x and y, what we get

00:28:30.820 --> 00:28:42.860
is 2 mu equal to 2,
which essentially

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:47.540
means that mu is
equal to negative 4

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:52.220
over 5, which is not
possible because we

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:53.880
want to choose to be positive.

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:54.760
Well, non-negative.

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:59.680
So this is not
what we need here.

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:15.240
All right.

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:19.820
And so we try with a
second case, case 2,

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:22.960
which is where we assume that
the constraint is now inactive,

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:30.180
meaning 2x plus y is
strictly less than 2.

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:32.860
Now, in this case, we know that
for all inactive constraints,

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:35.060
the mu's are equal to 0.

00:29:35.060 --> 00:29:39.500
And so our gradient of the
Lagrangian essentially becomes

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:48.020
2x to y plus 0, because this is
mu times the gradient of x of g,

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:54.740
which essentially means
that x and y are equal to 0.

00:29:54.740 --> 00:29:57.180
Now, this satisfies
the constraint

00:29:57.180 --> 00:30:02.180
because if we plug these values
here, it is indeed less than 2.

00:30:02.180 --> 00:30:06.980
And so this is indeed a
candidate for a local optimum.

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:08.260
And to convince you that--

00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:10.760
I mean, this was not
like a hard problem,

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:12.780
because-- just to
convince you that what

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:16.910
we did makes sense, what
we are characterizing here

00:30:16.910 --> 00:30:23.070
is a problem where we
wanted to basically satisfy

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:26.430
this inequality
constraint, which

00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:29.350
defines this line over here.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:32.910
And so the feasible region
is in this part of the line.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:36.630
And our objective
function is like a ball

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:38.410
that originates in 0 0.

00:30:42.010 --> 00:30:45.230
And so indeed, the
local minimum here--

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:50.270
well, global minimum
in this case is 0 0.

00:30:50.270 --> 00:30:51.210
Yes?

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:52.950
I apologize if I
missed it before.

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:56.670
Could you explain why
we use 0 in case 2?

00:30:56.670 --> 00:30:58.750
So here, we defined that--

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:02.750
if you remember how we
derived the KKT conditions,

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:05.190
is that we essentially
define the problem

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:08.090
to be identical to the equality.

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:10.530
And so we derived this
condition over here,

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:13.390
which could be
equivalently rewritten such

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:16.110
that we were
iterating or summing

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:20.150
over all inequality conditions,
inequality constraints,

00:31:20.150 --> 00:31:22.350
with the additional
condition that the mu has

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:25.350
to be 0 for all the
inactive constraints.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.510
And so in this case,
we were in a case

00:31:27.510 --> 00:31:30.110
where we were assuming this
constraint is inactive.

00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:33.110
And so for this constraint,
the mu has to be 0.

00:31:33.110 --> 00:31:36.610
So when the constraint
is inactive here,

00:31:36.610 --> 00:31:37.830
the mu is equal to 0.

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:39.230
Yes, correct.

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:41.047
Again, here, what
we're saying is that--

00:31:41.047 --> 00:31:42.630
this is an example
of how we could use

00:31:42.630 --> 00:31:45.090
these optimality conditions.

00:31:45.090 --> 00:31:46.550
So we did all of
this work to say--

00:31:46.550 --> 00:31:50.910
to come up with a condition
that we can use to potentially

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:52.470
filter for candidates.

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:53.290
So we use this.

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:55.730
We find potential
candidates for optimality.

00:31:55.730 --> 00:31:58.973
And then we check if
it makes sense or not.

00:31:58.973 --> 00:31:59.473
Yes?

00:31:59.473 --> 00:32:02.125
Why should mu be non-negative
for the [? active ?]

00:32:02.125 --> 00:32:03.470
constraints?

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:04.810
Yes, this is the point I was--

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:06.810
I'm happy to share with
you some literature.

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:08.350
It gets a little bit tricky.

00:32:08.350 --> 00:32:10.570
There are some geometrical
interpretations,

00:32:10.570 --> 00:32:12.550
but they're beyond the
scope of this class.

00:32:12.550 --> 00:32:13.897
Yes?

00:32:13.897 --> 00:32:15.230
Is there a multiple [INAUDIBLE]?

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:20.070
Exactly.

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:21.830
This is where it
gets tricky in here.

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:23.570
We use only one constraint.

00:32:23.570 --> 00:32:24.070
Yeah.

00:32:26.910 --> 00:32:30.943
So in case 1, it would have to
be greater than or equal to 0.

00:32:30.943 --> 00:32:35.020
[INAUDIBLE] positive,
strictly positive.

00:32:35.020 --> 00:32:39.390
In case 1, mu would have
to be strictly positive.

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:41.960
Well, no, right?

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:45.138
Because here, what we're
saying is that the-- so these

00:32:45.138 --> 00:32:45.930
are the conditions.

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:49.590
So we're just applying
the conditions nominally.

00:32:49.590 --> 00:32:53.590
And basically, the conditions
entail that for all constraints,

00:32:53.590 --> 00:32:57.950
the mu has to be non-negative,
except for the ones that

00:32:57.950 --> 00:33:01.030
are inactive, where they
actually have to be 0.

00:33:01.030 --> 00:33:03.630
Yes?

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:05.750
So am I understanding
it correctly

00:33:05.750 --> 00:33:09.150
that the conclusion that we
drew from check in case 1

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:12.670
is that there just does not
exist a candidate for optimality

00:33:12.670 --> 00:33:15.590
that has this active concern?

00:33:15.590 --> 00:33:17.270
Yeah, correct.

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:19.230
Again, here is
one simple example

00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:21.790
of how you could use optimality
conditions just as a way

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:23.750
to filter out candidates.

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:26.190
Yeah.

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:27.030
OK.

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:35.750
And so this actually wraps
the content for lecture 2.

00:33:35.750 --> 00:33:37.450
So let me just grab--

00:33:41.490 --> 00:33:41.990
OK.

00:33:41.990 --> 00:33:45.170
Any other questions on
this before we move on?

00:33:48.590 --> 00:33:50.190
Perfect.

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:53.790
And so with this,
now, we essentially

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:56.830
laid the foundation
of some intuitions

00:33:56.830 --> 00:33:59.630
that relate with finite
dimensional optimization.

00:33:59.630 --> 00:34:02.030
And now, we are--
and this is important

00:34:02.030 --> 00:34:04.630
because we will be coming
back to these ideas

00:34:04.630 --> 00:34:06.990
over and over in this
class, at the very least

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:10.870
for the optimal part
of this class before we

00:34:10.870 --> 00:34:13.030
go into learning.

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:17.429
And now, we start essentially
exploring our roadmap.

00:34:17.429 --> 00:34:18.989
And this is a slide
we will be coming

00:34:18.989 --> 00:34:21.750
to over and over because
it just helps you,

00:34:21.750 --> 00:34:26.070
I think, in grounding what
we're covering exactly.

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:29.150
And so specifically for the
next two to three weeks,

00:34:29.150 --> 00:34:32.530
we will be focused on this
quadrant of our roadmap.

00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:35.909
That is open loop methods
for optimal control, which

00:34:35.909 --> 00:34:39.949
are to be differentiated from
closed loop methods, which

00:34:39.949 --> 00:34:43.750
entail finding a closed
loop policy for behavior.

00:34:43.750 --> 00:34:48.270
And specifically for the next
three lectures within the class

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:51.610
of open loop methods, we'll be
looking at indirect methods,

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:55.110
which is historically how
optimal control started back

00:34:55.110 --> 00:35:00.630
in the '60s and '70s, which--
and so we will look at indirect

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:03.530
methods and then move towards
direct methods, which,

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:06.230
on the other hand, are
the more modern and way--

00:35:06.230 --> 00:35:09.610
modern way to solve optimal
control problems in practice.

00:35:12.230 --> 00:35:14.790
So what are indirect methods?

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:17.270
So at the high level,
indirect methods

00:35:17.270 --> 00:35:20.830
are essentially a mechanism
for which we basically

00:35:20.830 --> 00:35:23.870
go and define and determine
necessary optimality

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:28.110
conditions for optimal control
problems and solve for those.

00:35:28.110 --> 00:35:30.310
So if you wish,
everything that we

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:33.170
have been doing so far in
the finite dimensional case.

00:35:33.170 --> 00:35:36.750
So we have defined these optimal
control problems depending on

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:38.810
if they're constrained or not.

00:35:38.810 --> 00:35:42.190
We derived optimality
conditions and used as we just

00:35:42.190 --> 00:35:44.750
did, for example, these
optimality conditions to filter

00:35:44.750 --> 00:35:48.430
out candidates for
optimal, for minimums.

00:35:48.430 --> 00:35:50.670
In the exact same
way, indirect methods

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:53.370
try to use the exact
same formalism.

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:55.390
That is, define
optimality conditions

00:35:55.390 --> 00:35:57.630
and use those to
filter out solutions,

00:35:57.630 --> 00:35:59.450
this time for the
original problem,

00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:05.110
which is the one we were looking
at the beginning, which is

00:36:05.110 --> 00:36:06.690
the one in infinite dimensions.

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:16.790
So we are looking at the
dynamical system, which

00:36:16.790 --> 00:36:21.410
is with the objective function
that we were describing before.

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:30.790
And what I mean by the
fact that this is actually

00:36:30.790 --> 00:36:32.550
an infinite dimensional
optimization

00:36:32.550 --> 00:36:36.350
problem is because while
here kind of variables

00:36:36.350 --> 00:36:38.730
are essentially
vectors, in this case,

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:44.230
we are interested in optimizing
kind of a full signal u of t

00:36:44.230 --> 00:36:48.390
in between time t0 and tf.

00:36:48.390 --> 00:36:51.910
So you should imagine
that for us, u of t

00:36:51.910 --> 00:36:58.030
is the full signal that we want
to optimize, not a single point.

00:36:58.030 --> 00:36:59.530
OK.

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:02.230
And indirect methods
are also known

00:37:02.230 --> 00:37:04.130
as optimized then discretized.

00:37:04.130 --> 00:37:06.150
And this is because
optimize refers

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:09.190
to deriving optimality
conditions, which

00:37:09.190 --> 00:37:11.270
kind of as a
spoiler, in the case

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:13.450
of infinite
dimensional problems,

00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:16.230
these optimality conditions,
as we will see today,

00:37:16.230 --> 00:37:20.830
will essentially end up
being differential equations.

00:37:20.830 --> 00:37:23.330
And then discretized because
these differential equations,

00:37:23.330 --> 00:37:25.750
in order to solve them,
we typically feed them

00:37:25.750 --> 00:37:28.230
through solvers,
numerical solvers, which

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:30.910
entail some discretization.

00:37:30.910 --> 00:37:34.550
And these are to
be kind of compared

00:37:34.550 --> 00:37:36.770
to direct methods,
which, on the other hand,

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:40.510
are usually referred to as
discretized then optimize.

00:37:40.510 --> 00:37:42.470
So we are given
an optimal control

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:44.670
problem in continuous time.

00:37:44.670 --> 00:37:46.890
We discretize time
by, for example,

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:49.170
using a specific
discretization of time,

00:37:49.170 --> 00:37:51.470
replacing integrals with
sums-- with summations

00:37:51.470 --> 00:37:54.270
and so forth, and
then optimizing

00:37:54.270 --> 00:37:57.830
this finite-- this
discrete time problem.

00:37:57.830 --> 00:38:00.750
And so you can see how these
two methods are in a way

00:38:00.750 --> 00:38:04.190
nicely complementary.

00:38:04.190 --> 00:38:07.390
But so why do we
bother kind of going

00:38:07.390 --> 00:38:10.730
through these ancient tools,
such as indirect methods?

00:38:10.730 --> 00:38:14.950
And the point is that sometimes
indirect methods provide

00:38:14.950 --> 00:38:16.790
a more direct,
that is analytical

00:38:16.790 --> 00:38:19.790
or semi-analytical solution
for the optimal control

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:23.550
problem, which is great because
it means that at deployment

00:38:23.550 --> 00:38:26.150
on board, we have to do very
little computation, which

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:28.070
is great in the case
where we have only

00:38:28.070 --> 00:38:30.610
limited compute environments.

00:38:30.610 --> 00:38:35.030
For example, in aerospace,
these are highly, highly used.

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:37.230
And at the same time,
indirect methods

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:40.350
are also typically used to
maybe provide initial guesses

00:38:40.350 --> 00:38:42.550
for direct methods.

00:38:42.550 --> 00:38:45.370
And so the message here is
that I'm not wasting your time.

00:38:49.230 --> 00:38:51.270
So these techniques
are usually--

00:38:51.270 --> 00:38:54.390
can be quite
mathematically challenging.

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:58.230
And this is because, kind of
as a result of this, books

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:00.190
and textbooks and
material is generally

00:39:00.190 --> 00:39:02.810
written by mathematicians,
which is great.

00:39:02.810 --> 00:39:04.790
They're perfect, but
also sometimes they

00:39:04.790 --> 00:39:09.250
do not really follow the
desiderata of control engineers.

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:11.070
And so I definitely
advise you to go

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:12.570
through this
reference over here,

00:39:12.570 --> 00:39:14.650
which I think strikes
a nice middle ground.

00:39:18.050 --> 00:39:18.550
OK.

00:39:18.550 --> 00:39:21.710
So in general, so we were
referring to this problem

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:24.110
before, but so now, we
are taking the first step

00:39:24.110 --> 00:39:26.390
into tackling the
optimal control problem

00:39:26.390 --> 00:39:28.870
kind of at first hand.

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:31.110
And again, what we're
saying is that we

00:39:31.110 --> 00:39:33.590
want to find an admissible
control u star, which

00:39:33.590 --> 00:39:36.230
causes the dynamical system
defined by a differential

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:40.390
equation to follow an admissible
trajectory x of stars that

00:39:40.390 --> 00:39:43.330
minimizes the functional j.

00:39:46.190 --> 00:39:49.970
And what we saw last week,
and at the beginning today,

00:39:49.970 --> 00:39:52.550
is that for a function,
we essentially

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:55.230
defined optimality
conditions that boil down

00:39:55.230 --> 00:39:58.350
to setting the gradient to
0 to find stationary points,

00:39:58.350 --> 00:40:01.190
and then either using higher
order derivatives to determine

00:40:01.190 --> 00:40:04.110
whether these are
maximums or minimums,

00:40:04.110 --> 00:40:07.510
or just iterate through
all of the candidates

00:40:07.510 --> 00:40:10.830
and check if these
are feasible, and what

00:40:10.830 --> 00:40:14.150
is the minimum among
these candidates.

00:40:14.150 --> 00:40:19.110
We'll do something very
similar for function out, which

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:22.410
is the extension of a function
in infinite dimensions.

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:27.510
So loosely speaking,
functionals can be thought

00:40:27.510 --> 00:40:32.690
of as functions over functions.

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:40.350
And again, very mechanically
what we mean by this

00:40:40.350 --> 00:40:44.250
is that u of t, in this
case, is an entire signal,

00:40:44.250 --> 00:40:47.150
an entire function, as
we were saying before,

00:40:47.150 --> 00:40:49.810
and j, which in this
case is a functional,

00:40:49.810 --> 00:40:52.990
is something that takes
as input a full function

00:40:52.990 --> 00:40:57.270
and spits out, in this
case, a scalar number, which

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:59.710
is a measure of optimality.

00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:07.390
And the tool I'll be using today
to introduce these and derive

00:41:07.390 --> 00:41:09.990
these optimality
conditions is referred

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:12.270
to as calculus of
variations, which

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:14.310
is a generalization
of calculus that

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:17.690
allows to find maxima and
minima of functionals,

00:41:17.690 --> 00:41:19.770
specifically by
using variations.

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:22.330
And we'll see more
in the next slides

00:41:22.330 --> 00:41:25.830
what we mean by variations.

00:41:25.830 --> 00:41:28.270
Calculus of variations
is even more ancient

00:41:28.270 --> 00:41:29.710
than indirect methods.

00:41:29.710 --> 00:41:32.390
It dates back 300, 400 years.

00:41:32.390 --> 00:41:34.750
And it's really kind
of a fundamental kind

00:41:34.750 --> 00:41:38.030
of subfield of calculus.

00:41:38.030 --> 00:41:41.470
And once again,
these topics can be

00:41:41.470 --> 00:41:44.390
extremely heavy from a
mathematical standpoint.

00:41:44.390 --> 00:41:47.630
And so in order to
be focused on what

00:41:47.630 --> 00:41:50.630
is the goal for us in this
class, what I'll try to do here

00:41:50.630 --> 00:41:54.110
is that I'll essentially
be constantly mapping

00:41:54.110 --> 00:41:56.310
between the results
that we want to obtain

00:41:56.310 --> 00:41:58.160
in the infinite
dimensional case,

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:01.280
that is, through calculus of
variation, to the intuitions

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:04.240
that we built in the
finite dimensional case,

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:06.360
in order that is probably
the setting we are all

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:08.760
most familiar with.

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:11.742
And specifically,
we will start by--

00:42:11.742 --> 00:42:13.200
so if you look at
this agenda, this

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:15.280
is essentially the
one-to-one mapping

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:18.400
on what we did on
last week Monday.

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:23.760
So we'll start by introducing
concepts for functionals.

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:26.280
For example, defining
what is a functional,

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:30.640
what it means for two functions
to be close to each other, what

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:34.840
it means to be a minimum or an
extreme point for functionals.

00:42:34.840 --> 00:42:37.440
And then we'll
use this to derive

00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:40.560
what is referred to as the
fundamental theorem of calculus

00:42:40.560 --> 00:42:42.280
of variations.

00:42:42.280 --> 00:42:44.920
That is the necessary
and optimality conditions

00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:47.080
for the infinite
dimensional problem.

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:51.640
And then we will apply
this to optimal control.

00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:56.460
Any questions on the plan or
questions so far in general?

00:43:03.400 --> 00:43:05.792
OK.

00:43:05.792 --> 00:43:06.540
All right.

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:08.800
So what I'll be doing
for the next 15 minutes

00:43:08.800 --> 00:43:12.080
is probably a bit boring, but
it introduces the terminology

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:14.840
and notation that we
need going forward.

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:17.080
And so specifically,
a functional j

00:43:17.080 --> 00:43:20.920
is a rule of correspondence
that assigns to each function

00:43:20.920 --> 00:43:23.960
x in a certain class
omega, say the class

00:43:23.960 --> 00:43:28.800
of continuous functions,
a unique real number.

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:31.500
And it is always useful to
look through an example.

00:43:31.500 --> 00:43:34.480
So for example, a
possible functional

00:43:34.480 --> 00:43:37.000
is this functional
j of x, which is

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:39.240
the integral between t0 and tf.

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:41.580
T0 is just a number say, 3tf.

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:46.280
Again, just the number say 6
of x of t in dt, where x of t

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:50.480
could be any continuous
function, for example.

00:43:50.480 --> 00:43:54.240
And a specific class of
functionals we are interested in

00:43:54.240 --> 00:43:58.760
is the class of
linear functions,

00:43:58.760 --> 00:44:01.480
which are defined by
the fact that they

00:44:01.480 --> 00:44:03.380
respect two properties.

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:05.800
The first one is homogeneity.

00:44:05.800 --> 00:44:10.380
So the functional of the
scaled version of a function,

00:44:10.380 --> 00:44:14.320
so here alpha times x, is
equal to the scaled version

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:18.180
of the function, so
alpha times g of x.

00:44:24.640 --> 00:44:25.600
Homogeneity.

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:33.860
OK, thanks.

00:44:39.680 --> 00:44:41.860
And the second
one is additivity.

00:44:45.680 --> 00:44:47.760
That is the
functional of the sum

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:55.720
of two functions is equal to the
sum of the functionals applied

00:44:55.720 --> 00:45:03.720
to the individual functions,
which together lead

00:45:03.720 --> 00:45:05.620
to the superposition property.

00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:12.400
So, as an example, the
previous functional

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:15.780
is linear because
say that we are--

00:45:21.960 --> 00:45:26.000
if you want to do j of alpha
x, which essentially means

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:33.920
the integral between t0 and
tf of alpha x of t in the t,

00:45:33.920 --> 00:45:45.360
we can carry out the
alpha from the integral,

00:45:45.360 --> 00:45:51.080
which is equivalent to
saying alpha j of x.

00:45:51.080 --> 00:45:54.280
Again, nothing deep
here, but just proving

00:45:54.280 --> 00:45:58.400
that the example here is indeed
respecting the homogeneity,

00:45:58.400 --> 00:45:58.900
for example.

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:05.260
And you can do an equally
simple proof for additivity.

00:46:05.260 --> 00:46:05.760
OK.

00:46:05.760 --> 00:46:06.820
So next.

00:46:06.820 --> 00:46:10.440
So remember that
to define what is

00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:13.040
a minimum in the finite
dimensional case,

00:46:13.040 --> 00:46:17.580
we use the idea of being
optimal in a neighborhood.

00:46:17.580 --> 00:46:19.560
And to define neighborhood,
we essentially

00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:23.060
needed to have the concept
of closeness, right?

00:46:23.060 --> 00:46:27.120
And to define closeness in
the case of functionals,

00:46:27.120 --> 00:46:30.200
we resort to a concept
that is exactly

00:46:30.200 --> 00:46:32.200
the one we use also in
finite dimensions, which

00:46:32.200 --> 00:46:34.160
is one of norms.

00:46:34.160 --> 00:46:39.840
And so specifically the norm
in the case of a function

00:46:39.840 --> 00:46:42.400
is a rule of
correspondence that assigns

00:46:42.400 --> 00:46:45.400
to each function x, again,
in a specific domain

00:46:45.400 --> 00:46:48.360
omega, a real number.

00:46:48.360 --> 00:46:52.900
And it is defined as we
do in finite dimensions,

00:46:52.900 --> 00:46:54.860
and it satisfies the
following properties.

00:46:54.860 --> 00:47:00.880
That is the norm of a function
has to be non-negative.

00:47:00.880 --> 00:47:04.100
And it has to be equal--

00:47:04.100 --> 00:47:07.240
and the norm is of a
function is equal to 0

00:47:07.240 --> 00:47:12.800
if and only if the function
is equal to 0 for all times.

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:18.000
The second property is that
the norm of the scaled function

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:22.480
is equal to the absolute value
of the scaling factor multiplied

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:26.600
by the norm of that function
for all real numbers alpha,

00:47:26.600 --> 00:47:31.120
and it has to respect the
triangular inequality.

00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:34.860
And in practice to compare
closeness between two functions,

00:47:34.860 --> 00:47:39.000
say y and z, what
we do is we let

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:42.220
x be the difference
between the two functions,

00:47:42.220 --> 00:47:46.640
and we measure the norm
of that difference.

00:47:46.640 --> 00:47:48.997
So it might seem, as
we are doing nothing,

00:47:48.997 --> 00:47:51.080
but what we are essentially
doing is just carrying

00:47:51.080 --> 00:47:52.340
over all of these results.

00:47:52.340 --> 00:47:54.120
We have infinite dimensional--

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:58.840
infinite dimensions to the
infinite dimensional case.

00:47:58.840 --> 00:48:04.960
As an example, consider
the continuous functions x.

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:07.760
A possible definition
of the norm

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:10.480
could be the maximum
between t that

00:48:10.480 --> 00:48:15.680
goes from t0 to tf of the
absolute value of x of t.

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:22.000
And you can see that this does
indeed satisfy these properties.

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:25.160
For example, the
value of the norm

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:27.660
is, by definition,
always non-negative,

00:48:27.660 --> 00:48:30.840
because we're taking the
maximum absolute value.

00:48:30.840 --> 00:48:37.020
And the only way the norm is
0 if it is 0 across all times,

00:48:37.020 --> 00:48:39.360
because we're indeed
taking the maximum.

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:41.120
So this is for the
first property.

00:48:41.120 --> 00:48:43.900
But indeed, I do invite you to
check the other two properties.

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:47.120
But just as an example
of what is a norm for us.

00:48:47.120 --> 00:48:48.080
Yeah?

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:51.622
So to set omega, the
functions in that set

00:48:51.622 --> 00:48:54.080
have to be related to each
other at all, or is it something

00:48:54.080 --> 00:48:55.980
that we choose individually?

00:48:55.980 --> 00:48:58.220
No, I mean, omega here is
pretty general definition.

00:48:58.220 --> 00:49:00.640
It could be the set of
continuous functions.

00:49:00.640 --> 00:49:01.840
Yeah.

00:49:01.840 --> 00:49:03.380
So in this case,
they are related,

00:49:03.380 --> 00:49:07.940
but I would say just a
domain for our functions.

00:49:07.940 --> 00:49:09.120
Yeah?

00:49:09.120 --> 00:49:10.840
Could you help bridge the gap?

00:49:10.840 --> 00:49:17.345
There's a concept here of
closeness but also [INAUDIBLE].

00:49:17.345 --> 00:49:21.080
Why are they connecting
closeness [INAUDIBLE]?

00:49:21.080 --> 00:49:22.320
Yeah, this is a good point.

00:49:22.320 --> 00:49:27.800
So essentially, the way
we do in finite dimensions

00:49:27.800 --> 00:49:31.880
is we use norm kind of loosely
speaking, to measure kind

00:49:31.880 --> 00:49:33.580
of distances between vectors.

00:49:33.580 --> 00:49:36.600
So usually what works
in infinite dimensions

00:49:36.600 --> 00:49:39.880
is literally to revert,
resort to your intuition

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:42.280
in finite dimensions
and trying to map it

00:49:42.280 --> 00:49:43.940
to the infinite
dimensional case.

00:49:43.940 --> 00:49:46.140
And so here, we're doing
exactly the same thing.

00:49:46.140 --> 00:49:48.720
We're using norms
over functions, not

00:49:48.720 --> 00:49:53.120
over vectors to measure kind
of distance between functions.

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:55.840
And we can use this distance
to measure, quote unquote,

00:49:55.840 --> 00:49:57.240
"closeness".

00:49:57.240 --> 00:49:59.700
And this will become more
practical in the next slides,

00:49:59.700 --> 00:50:02.680
but this is more or
less what we're doing.

00:50:02.680 --> 00:50:03.640
Does it make sense?

00:50:03.640 --> 00:50:04.320
Yeah.

00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:05.440
OK.

00:50:05.440 --> 00:50:05.960
Yeah?

00:50:05.960 --> 00:50:08.640
Just to clarify,
the norm is being

00:50:08.640 --> 00:50:11.380
taken of the difference
of the two functions,

00:50:11.380 --> 00:50:12.920
not on each function.

00:50:12.920 --> 00:50:13.900
Yes, correct.

00:50:16.620 --> 00:50:17.120
OK.

00:50:17.120 --> 00:50:20.620
So we're getting towards
what we actually care about.

00:50:20.620 --> 00:50:24.848
There is this defining
extrema for functions.

00:50:24.848 --> 00:50:26.640
So now that we have a
measure of closeness,

00:50:26.640 --> 00:50:28.520
we can actually do the
exact same thing we're

00:50:28.520 --> 00:50:30.060
doing in finite dimensions.

00:50:30.060 --> 00:50:36.320
And specifically, a functional
j has a local minimum

00:50:36.320 --> 00:50:38.620
at x star of t.

00:50:38.620 --> 00:50:41.440
Again, x star of
t is a full signal

00:50:41.440 --> 00:50:44.800
just so that we don't get
confused as we would do

00:50:44.800 --> 00:50:47.560
in the finite dimensional case.

00:50:47.560 --> 00:50:51.840
If there exists an
epsilon greater than 0

00:50:51.840 --> 00:50:56.480
such that for all
functions that are

00:50:56.480 --> 00:50:59.527
kind of close to the
local minimum, that

00:50:59.527 --> 00:51:01.360
is, the distance between
these two functions

00:51:01.360 --> 00:51:04.960
is smaller than this epsilon,
the value of the function

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:08.840
is greater or equal
than x of star.

00:51:08.840 --> 00:51:10.800
So again, exact
same result that we

00:51:10.800 --> 00:51:14.010
have in finite dimensions,
exact same means of getting

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:15.760
to the result, but
we're just extending it

00:51:15.760 --> 00:51:18.160
to the infinite
dimensional case.

00:51:18.160 --> 00:51:19.200
Yes?

00:51:19.200 --> 00:51:23.460
You quote it's
excellent to use signal.

00:51:23.460 --> 00:51:26.640
What if the signal be u?

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:27.820
Yes, this is a good point.

00:51:27.820 --> 00:51:30.600
So here we are
introducing these ideas

00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:33.360
from a high-level standpoint.

00:51:33.360 --> 00:51:36.160
For us, we are interested in
optimizing control signals,

00:51:36.160 --> 00:51:37.220
which is u.

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:40.380
So in general-- here, we're
using x as a general notation.

00:51:40.380 --> 00:51:42.980
But in practice, we will
always be optimizing.

00:51:46.700 --> 00:51:47.200
OK.

00:51:47.200 --> 00:51:49.880
And maxima, of course,
defined similarly

00:51:49.880 --> 00:51:54.720
just by flipping the sign.

00:51:54.720 --> 00:51:59.840
Now, to define minimum,
we need to define

00:51:59.840 --> 00:52:04.960
something that is similar to
the differential of a function.

00:52:04.960 --> 00:52:25.320
And just for-- as a
reminder, remember

00:52:25.320 --> 00:52:28.200
that to derive the necessary
optimality conditions

00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:31.480
in the finite dimensional
case, what we did

00:52:31.480 --> 00:52:33.480
is that we essentially
were looking

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:37.440
at the increment of a function.

00:52:37.440 --> 00:52:40.680
And we saw how this
could be approximated

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:45.040
by the value of
the function itself

00:52:45.040 --> 00:52:50.160
plus the gradient of
f transpose delta x

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:58.480
plus some higher order
terms, where this is what we

00:52:58.480 --> 00:53:01.940
called a differential, right?

00:53:01.940 --> 00:53:05.600
So this is exactly
what we did on Monday.

00:53:05.600 --> 00:53:09.120
And specifically,
the reason why we

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:10.800
did this is that
we used the fact

00:53:10.800 --> 00:53:16.560
that for small enough increments
delta x, the value of a function

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:19.720
is well approximated
by this linear part.

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:24.040
And then what we did is that we
essentially derived conditions

00:53:24.040 --> 00:53:26.360
on this linear part
to say something

00:53:26.360 --> 00:53:31.080
about the minimum
of the function.

00:53:31.080 --> 00:53:36.920
Specifically, we went from this
differential to the condition

00:53:36.920 --> 00:53:41.490
that at the minima, the gradient
of f needs to be equal to 0.

00:53:41.490 --> 00:53:44.130
So this is what
we did on Monday.

00:53:44.130 --> 00:53:46.130
And so intuitively,
what we want to do

00:53:46.130 --> 00:53:49.010
is something very similar,
actually, exactly the same,

00:53:49.010 --> 00:53:51.270
but again, in the
infinite dimensional case.

00:53:54.130 --> 00:53:57.010
And to do this, we
start by introducing

00:53:57.010 --> 00:54:00.530
an increment of a
function that is defined

00:54:00.530 --> 00:54:04.210
as the difference between
the value of the functional

00:54:04.210 --> 00:54:09.570
evaluated at the function
plus a variation of x

00:54:09.570 --> 00:54:14.270
minus the value of the function
at the function itself.

00:54:18.010 --> 00:54:23.830
And now is where I think, again,
most of the brainpower goes.

00:54:26.948 --> 00:54:28.990
So at the high-level, this
is what we want to do.

00:54:28.990 --> 00:54:31.770
So we want to follow
exactly the same recipe that

00:54:31.770 --> 00:54:35.850
is defined something
similar to a differential

00:54:35.850 --> 00:54:40.490
and use this linear part that
well approximates our function

00:54:40.490 --> 00:54:43.330
to derive a condition
on this linear part

00:54:43.330 --> 00:54:46.330
in the infinite
dimensional case.

00:54:46.330 --> 00:54:52.290
To do this, we need to define
a notion of differentiability.

00:54:52.290 --> 00:54:52.970
Yes?

00:54:52.970 --> 00:54:55.890
The comma is supposed
to be plus straight?

00:54:55.890 --> 00:54:56.770
Sorry?

00:54:56.770 --> 00:54:59.650
The x of t comma
delta x of t, that's

00:54:59.650 --> 00:55:02.050
supposed to be a plus straight?

00:55:02.050 --> 00:55:04.150
That's what you
thought or is it not--

00:55:04.150 --> 00:55:05.350
No, this is correct.

00:55:05.350 --> 00:55:07.010
So we are defining
the increment,

00:55:07.010 --> 00:55:11.450
which we express
basically as a function

00:55:11.450 --> 00:55:16.050
of-- the original function x
of t and the variation delta.

00:55:16.050 --> 00:55:18.970
So that equals what
you were talking

00:55:18.970 --> 00:55:22.570
about x plus delta x, right?

00:55:22.570 --> 00:55:28.010
No, this increment
would be f of--

00:55:28.010 --> 00:55:30.370
So it's a completely
different function than the j

00:55:30.370 --> 00:55:32.542
that you were
talking about before?

00:55:32.542 --> 00:55:33.750
No, it's this part over here.

00:55:33.750 --> 00:55:35.333
So if we bring this
on the other side,

00:55:35.333 --> 00:55:37.090
this is the increment
because it's

00:55:37.090 --> 00:55:39.350
the difference of the
value of the function,

00:55:39.350 --> 00:55:42.250
and this is the difference of
the value of the functionals.

00:55:42.250 --> 00:55:44.370
The function else?

00:55:44.370 --> 00:55:45.350
Functionals.

00:55:45.350 --> 00:55:45.890
Functionals?

00:55:45.890 --> 00:55:47.370
Yes.

00:55:47.370 --> 00:55:48.310
Does that make sense?

00:55:51.150 --> 00:55:51.650
OK.

00:55:51.650 --> 00:55:53.410
So here, what we're
essentially doing

00:55:53.410 --> 00:55:57.650
is expanding the concept
of differentiability.

00:55:57.650 --> 00:56:04.010
And to do this, we first rewrite
the increment in this way.

00:56:04.010 --> 00:56:06.690
And specifically, the
increment we rewrite it

00:56:06.690 --> 00:56:14.490
as the sum of delta j, which is
a linear part plus some stuff.

00:56:14.490 --> 00:56:18.690
And some stuff is the equivalent
of the higher order terms

00:56:18.690 --> 00:56:20.650
in the finite dimensional case.

00:56:20.650 --> 00:56:22.540
And so here, you
might say, well, sure,

00:56:22.540 --> 00:56:24.290
we can always write
something as something

00:56:24.290 --> 00:56:26.410
linear plus some stuff.

00:56:26.410 --> 00:56:30.930
The point is that to define
what it means for a function

00:56:30.930 --> 00:56:35.650
to be differentiable, this
stuff has a specific property.

00:56:35.650 --> 00:56:37.250
And specifically
what we're saying

00:56:37.250 --> 00:56:44.450
is that if this part g goes
to the limit of smaller

00:56:44.450 --> 00:56:47.930
and smaller delta
x goes to 0, we

00:56:47.930 --> 00:56:50.810
have something that
goes to 0 times

00:56:50.810 --> 00:56:52.810
something that also
goes to 0, which

00:56:52.810 --> 00:56:56.970
means that this higher order
terms goes quadratically to 0.

00:56:56.970 --> 00:57:00.530
Well, this is a linear
term, linear in delta

00:57:00.530 --> 00:57:04.490
x, which means that
goes only linearly to 0,

00:57:04.490 --> 00:57:09.970
which means that the increment
of the functional for smaller

00:57:09.970 --> 00:57:16.450
delta x is well approximated
by only this linear part.

00:57:16.450 --> 00:57:19.987
So again, we're trying to
do the exact same statement

00:57:19.987 --> 00:57:22.070
that we were doing in the
finite dimensional case.

00:57:25.330 --> 00:57:28.690
And so again, so this is the
notion of differentiability,

00:57:28.690 --> 00:57:30.570
meaning that given
that we are rewriting

00:57:30.570 --> 00:57:34.410
the increment in this way,
if this condition is true,

00:57:34.410 --> 00:57:38.330
then the functional j is said
to be differentiable on x.

00:57:38.330 --> 00:57:41.410
And delta j, so
this term over here,

00:57:41.410 --> 00:57:44.190
is referred to as the
variation of j at x.

00:57:50.170 --> 00:57:52.730
Any questions here?

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:53.690
Yeah?

00:57:53.690 --> 00:57:56.408
Is this definition coming
from the Taylor series

00:57:56.408 --> 00:57:57.950
or is this just
completely different?

00:57:57.950 --> 00:57:59.190
Can you repeat louder?

00:57:59.190 --> 00:58:00.793
Is this definition
for the increment

00:58:00.793 --> 00:58:03.210
coming from the Taylor series
you're talking about before,

00:58:03.210 --> 00:58:04.590
or is this completely different?

00:58:04.590 --> 00:58:09.430
It's a great question,
because in a way,

00:58:09.430 --> 00:58:11.430
everything that I'm saying
here is s actionable.

00:58:11.430 --> 00:58:14.230
Meaning I haven't told you
how to get any of this.

00:58:14.230 --> 00:58:17.410
And so we'll see later in the
next few slides how to do this.

00:58:17.410 --> 00:58:20.730
So here, what we're only doing
is setting the stage for that

00:58:20.730 --> 00:58:23.157
result. Specifically
by setting the stage,

00:58:23.157 --> 00:58:25.490
I mean that we are trying to
draw parallels between what

00:58:25.490 --> 00:58:27.690
we're doing in the
finite dimensional case,

00:58:27.690 --> 00:58:32.890
and here expanding the
notion of differentiability.

00:58:32.890 --> 00:58:34.030
Was that a question or no?

00:58:34.030 --> 00:58:34.530
Yes.

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:37.050
So for now, we'll just
say it's unrelated.

00:58:37.050 --> 00:58:41.010
For now, it's just a result
that is taking a step

00:58:41.010 --> 00:58:45.490
into how we actually compute.

00:58:45.490 --> 00:58:46.130
Yes?

00:58:46.130 --> 00:58:46.630
Sorry.

00:58:46.630 --> 00:58:50.350
[INAUDIBLE] by what
the [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:50.350 --> 00:58:50.850
Yeah.

00:58:50.850 --> 00:58:54.690
Again, so here, I have
not defined concretely

00:58:54.690 --> 00:58:57.730
what any of this is.

00:58:57.730 --> 00:59:00.890
Intuitively, you can think
of this part over here

00:59:00.890 --> 00:59:04.170
as the equivalent
of the higher order

00:59:04.170 --> 00:59:06.470
terms in our Taylor expansion.

00:59:06.470 --> 00:59:06.970
Yeah.

00:59:10.450 --> 00:59:10.950
OK.

00:59:10.950 --> 00:59:13.450
And so this-- once
we have this, we

00:59:13.450 --> 00:59:15.890
can formally state the
fundamental theorem

00:59:15.890 --> 00:59:19.650
of calculus of variations
or again, kind of i.e.

00:59:19.650 --> 00:59:23.490
The necessary and optimality
conditions for our infinite

00:59:23.490 --> 00:59:26.210
dimensional problem.

00:59:26.210 --> 00:59:28.390
Specifically, the
theorem goes as follows.

00:59:28.390 --> 00:59:33.730
So let x of t be a vector
function of t in the class

00:59:33.730 --> 00:59:38.610
omega and j of x be a
differentiable function of x.

00:59:38.610 --> 00:59:41.650
Again, differentiable meaning
that the increment is well

00:59:41.650 --> 00:59:44.930
approximated by the
linear term delta

00:59:44.930 --> 00:59:47.050
j that we were seeing before.

00:59:47.050 --> 00:59:49.290
Assume that the
functions in omega

00:59:49.290 --> 00:59:51.310
are not constrained
by any boundaries.

00:59:51.310 --> 00:59:53.650
For now, ignore this sentence.

00:59:53.650 --> 00:59:57.530
If x of star is an
extremal, the variation of j

00:59:57.530 --> 01:00:00.170
must vanish at x of star.

01:00:00.170 --> 01:00:03.450
That is, the necessary
optimality conditions

01:00:03.450 --> 01:00:08.970
for our problems is that the
variation of j at a local minima

01:00:08.970 --> 01:00:13.930
x star needs to be equal
to 0 for all admissible

01:00:13.930 --> 01:00:15.710
delta x is small enough.

01:00:18.770 --> 01:00:25.290
So again, here, we won't be
bringing forward the proof

01:00:25.290 --> 01:00:28.810
because it's fairly intricate
and in my opinion, not

01:00:28.810 --> 01:00:30.650
super insightful.

01:00:30.650 --> 01:00:34.010
But in a way, how I
want you to interpret

01:00:34.010 --> 01:00:36.290
this result is that
this is exactly

01:00:36.290 --> 01:00:42.650
the analog of the gradient
to f transpose delta

01:00:42.650 --> 01:00:47.530
x equal to 0 condition
that we saw on Monday.

01:00:47.530 --> 01:00:49.870
Now, in the finite
dimensional case,

01:00:49.870 --> 01:00:54.970
this was directly leading
towards an actionable thing

01:00:54.970 --> 01:00:57.290
we could compute, which
is the gradient of f

01:00:57.290 --> 01:00:59.130
equal to 0 condition.

01:00:59.130 --> 01:01:01.650
In this case, this is
not yet actionable,

01:01:01.650 --> 01:01:03.750
but we'll build towards it.

01:01:03.750 --> 01:01:05.810
But intuitively and
at a high-level,

01:01:05.810 --> 01:01:08.250
you should map
this result exactly

01:01:08.250 --> 01:01:10.290
to the differential
equal to 0 result

01:01:10.290 --> 01:01:13.050
in the finite dimensional case.

01:01:13.050 --> 01:01:14.850
Yes?

01:01:14.850 --> 01:01:17.170
You use a lower case delta j.

01:01:17.170 --> 01:01:21.530
In the biggest slide,
[INAUDIBLE] full delta j.

01:01:21.530 --> 01:01:22.630
That's the same thing?

01:01:22.630 --> 01:01:27.450
We're talking about this
linear part over here.

01:01:27.450 --> 01:01:29.970
So the capital delta
is the increment.

01:01:29.970 --> 01:01:33.810
So the difference between
the value of the function,

01:01:33.810 --> 01:01:36.450
well, plus the variation
minus the original function.

01:01:36.450 --> 01:01:43.930
And lower delta here is what we
introduced to be as a variation.

01:01:43.930 --> 01:01:46.010
Again, kind of in
your mental model,

01:01:46.010 --> 01:01:49.330
I would suggest you see
this as the equivalent

01:01:49.330 --> 01:01:50.530
of the differential.

01:01:50.530 --> 01:01:53.370
So it's the linear part
that approximates very well

01:01:53.370 --> 01:01:54.250
the increment.

01:01:57.450 --> 01:01:59.990
And in the finite
dimensional case,

01:01:59.990 --> 01:02:02.370
what we did is that once we
had this linear part that

01:02:02.370 --> 01:02:07.730
approximates well the increment,
we derived a condition

01:02:07.730 --> 01:02:10.090
on this linear part.

01:02:10.090 --> 01:02:13.450
Here, we're doing
the exact same thing.

01:02:13.450 --> 01:02:15.970
Does that make sense?

01:02:15.970 --> 01:02:18.410
Yeah.

01:02:18.410 --> 01:02:19.830
What is big?

01:02:19.830 --> 01:02:25.970
No, I'm not sure
I understand what

01:02:25.970 --> 01:02:29.410
the increment and
the linear part is.

01:02:29.410 --> 01:02:31.810
Why there's a difference?

01:02:31.810 --> 01:02:33.410
So the increment,
we're just defining

01:02:33.410 --> 01:02:35.330
as the difference
between the value

01:02:35.330 --> 01:02:39.370
of a functional at the
kind of function plus

01:02:39.370 --> 01:02:42.483
variation minus the value of
the function at the original--

01:02:42.483 --> 01:02:44.150
the functional at the
original function.

01:02:44.150 --> 01:02:47.450
So this is just a definition.

01:02:47.450 --> 01:02:51.250
Then what we're saying
is that we can--

01:02:51.250 --> 01:02:55.610
for variations of the function
delta x that are small enough,

01:02:55.610 --> 01:02:59.970
this increment is well
approximated by a linear part.

01:02:59.970 --> 01:03:03.090
And then we use this linear
part to basically impose

01:03:03.090 --> 01:03:06.570
a condition on this
linear part to derive

01:03:06.570 --> 01:03:09.530
our optimality conditions.

01:03:09.530 --> 01:03:13.050
Again, I understand how
this can look a bit vague,

01:03:13.050 --> 01:03:15.830
because here, it's still
not really actionable yet.

01:03:15.830 --> 01:03:17.510
In two slides, it will be.

01:03:20.310 --> 01:03:20.810
OK.

01:03:20.810 --> 01:03:23.170
But again, so
fundamental theorem

01:03:23.170 --> 01:03:26.970
of the calculus of
variations is the analog

01:03:26.970 --> 01:03:29.130
of the differential
equal to 0 condition

01:03:29.130 --> 01:03:30.850
in the finite
dimensional case, so

01:03:30.850 --> 01:03:33.050
the necessary
optimality conditions

01:03:33.050 --> 01:03:35.530
in the infinite
dimensional case.

01:03:35.530 --> 01:03:36.370
Yes?

01:03:36.370 --> 01:03:41.250
The delta x in the delta
j function there is not

01:03:41.250 --> 01:03:43.010
the variation of x over time?

01:03:43.010 --> 01:03:45.738
Yes, correct.

01:03:45.738 --> 01:03:47.030
Actually, this is a good point.

01:03:47.030 --> 01:03:53.750
Let me try and give some
intuition to what delta x is.

01:04:01.610 --> 01:04:11.990
So say that this is our x of
t, so our function x of t.

01:04:15.090 --> 01:04:19.650
We can define-- and
this is like t0.

01:04:19.650 --> 01:04:21.970
This is tf.

01:04:21.970 --> 01:04:25.772
Delta x could be a function.

01:04:25.772 --> 01:04:27.230
There could be
something like this.

01:04:27.230 --> 01:04:29.970
So delta x is, in
itself, a function,

01:04:29.970 --> 01:04:34.950
which leads to x plus delta
x being something like this.

01:04:34.950 --> 01:04:38.170
So this would be
delta x and this would

01:04:38.170 --> 01:04:43.610
be x of t plus delta x.

01:04:43.610 --> 01:04:48.250
And then j would still be all
of that flipped into, I guess,

01:04:48.250 --> 01:04:50.730
the integral that you're
talking about as the example?

01:04:50.730 --> 01:04:53.930
We didn't define what
delta j is here yet.

01:04:53.930 --> 01:04:59.130
You should just see it as
the linear part, the analog

01:04:59.130 --> 01:05:03.330
of the linear part that will
approximate the increment

01:05:03.330 --> 01:05:05.870
in the infinite
dimensional case.

01:05:05.870 --> 01:05:06.370
Yeah.

01:05:10.410 --> 01:05:10.910
OK.

01:05:16.130 --> 01:05:18.730
Now, let's make this actionable.

01:05:18.730 --> 01:05:22.700
And to do this, let's
start by applying it

01:05:22.700 --> 01:05:26.660
to basically an extremely
simplified optimal control

01:05:26.660 --> 01:05:30.140
problem, if you wish, the
simplest possible optimal

01:05:30.140 --> 01:05:32.180
control problem
we could think of.

01:05:32.180 --> 01:05:34.140
Specifically, we
consider the problem

01:05:34.140 --> 01:05:40.340
where we want to minimize
this functional, which

01:05:40.340 --> 01:05:42.820
is defined as the
integral between t0

01:05:42.820 --> 01:05:48.100
and tf of this function g,
which is a function of x,

01:05:48.100 --> 01:05:51.700
the derivative of x and n time.

01:05:51.700 --> 01:05:55.660
So going point to
the observation

01:05:55.660 --> 01:05:57.460
that your colleague
was doing before,

01:05:57.460 --> 01:06:01.020
here, you should interpret
this as we want--

01:06:01.020 --> 01:06:03.780
as if we are directly
controlling the state

01:06:03.780 --> 01:06:05.160
trajectory, for example.

01:06:05.160 --> 01:06:08.300
So there is no control,
but we are directly

01:06:08.300 --> 01:06:11.080
steering the state
trajectory of our system.

01:06:15.980 --> 01:06:20.580
And so some assumptions here is
that g is twice differentiable.

01:06:20.580 --> 01:06:23.780
We are assuming that
t0 and tf are fixed.

01:06:23.780 --> 01:06:27.540
Again, this is something
we will relax on Wednesday.

01:06:27.540 --> 01:06:30.180
But for now, we assume that the
initial time and the final time

01:06:30.180 --> 01:06:31.460
are fixed and given.

01:06:31.460 --> 01:06:35.960
Same thing for the state at the
initial time and the final time.

01:06:35.960 --> 01:06:38.540
X0 and xf are also fixed.

01:06:38.540 --> 01:06:43.940
So to basically recap
what we're doing,

01:06:43.940 --> 01:06:46.900
so far, we have introduced
the fundamental theorem

01:06:46.900 --> 01:06:50.340
of calculus of variations, which
is the optimality conditions

01:06:50.340 --> 01:06:54.420
in the infinite
dimensional case, which

01:06:54.420 --> 01:06:58.860
we said is not really
actionable and operational yet.

01:06:58.860 --> 01:07:01.460
So now, we will make it
operational by starting

01:07:01.460 --> 01:07:04.340
to look at a simplified
version of an optimal control

01:07:04.340 --> 01:07:06.180
problem, which we
will be building

01:07:06.180 --> 01:07:10.500
on Wednesday to until we reach
to the full fledged optimal

01:07:10.500 --> 01:07:11.480
control problem.

01:07:14.460 --> 01:07:21.860
So to do this, let's apply
the fundamental theorem

01:07:21.860 --> 01:07:26.020
of calculus of variation.

01:07:26.020 --> 01:07:32.260
And specifically, what we do
here is we write the increment.

01:07:32.260 --> 01:07:34.800
So here, we're not really
doing anything particular.

01:07:34.800 --> 01:07:38.260
We're just applying the
definition of an increment.

01:07:38.260 --> 01:07:41.380
And then we're
substituting our value of j

01:07:41.380 --> 01:07:43.500
from our optimal
control problem.

01:07:43.500 --> 01:07:45.940
So for us, the increment
will be defined

01:07:45.940 --> 01:07:50.820
as the integral between t0
and tf of g of x plus delta

01:07:50.820 --> 01:07:56.420
x, x dot plus delta
x dot t in the t

01:07:56.420 --> 01:08:01.100
minus the original function g.

01:08:01.100 --> 01:08:05.060
We can bring the second
integral inside the first

01:08:05.060 --> 01:08:07.440
and we get this
equation over here.

01:08:07.440 --> 01:08:09.840
So completely nothing new here.

01:08:09.840 --> 01:08:12.380
We're just applying
the definition

01:08:12.380 --> 01:08:15.580
of an increment with
respect to our definition

01:08:15.580 --> 01:08:19.279
of j in this problem,
which was this one here.

01:08:23.859 --> 01:08:26.020
OK, so just a note on notation.

01:08:26.020 --> 01:08:28.500
X dot is the
derivative of x of t

01:08:28.500 --> 01:08:32.220
and the t and delta x dot is
also the derivative of delta xt.

01:08:36.680 --> 01:08:37.180
OK.

01:08:37.180 --> 01:08:45.779
So remember what we said for the
fundamental theorem of calculus

01:08:45.779 --> 01:08:50.660
of variation is that we took
the expression of the increment.

01:08:50.660 --> 01:08:52.620
And then we said
that the increment

01:08:52.620 --> 01:08:58.660
would be equal to a linear
part delta j plus some stuff.

01:08:58.660 --> 01:09:02.100
Now, here, what we want to do
is from this equation here,

01:09:02.100 --> 01:09:07.899
we want to extract the linear
part of this function over here.

01:09:07.899 --> 01:09:15.120
Now, in general,
assume a fixed t.

01:09:15.120 --> 01:09:17.819
So we are considering only
if you want one element

01:09:17.819 --> 01:09:19.979
of this integration.

01:09:19.979 --> 01:09:22.300
What is the tool
we would usually

01:09:22.300 --> 01:09:26.600
use to derive a kind of the
linear part in this case?

01:09:29.350 --> 01:09:30.700
Taylor series.

01:09:30.700 --> 01:09:31.600
Yes, exactly.

01:09:31.600 --> 01:09:32.580
Correct.

01:09:32.580 --> 01:09:35.479
So we will be using Taylor
over and over in this class.

01:09:35.479 --> 01:09:37.359
And so this is exactly
what we do here.

01:09:37.359 --> 01:09:40.859
So we basically do
the Taylor expansion

01:09:40.859 --> 01:09:46.340
of the term inside the
integral, whereby essentially,

01:09:46.340 --> 01:09:49.740
the increment becomes the
integral between t0 and tf of g

01:09:49.740 --> 01:09:55.900
x, x dot t, plus the derivative
of g with respect to x, which we

01:09:55.900 --> 01:10:01.540
refer to as g of x
times the variation in x

01:10:01.540 --> 01:10:05.260
plus the derivative of g
with respect to x dot times

01:10:05.260 --> 01:10:09.620
the variation in x dot plus
some higher order terms.

01:10:09.620 --> 01:10:12.960
So here, we just expanded
the first term, if you wish.

01:10:19.580 --> 01:10:23.940
Now, if you look at this, the
first term and the last term

01:10:23.940 --> 01:10:25.180
cancel out.

01:10:25.180 --> 01:10:28.980
And so what we are
left with is only

01:10:28.980 --> 01:10:32.660
with these three
terms over here.

01:10:32.660 --> 01:10:38.380
And even by inspection,
what would you say--

01:10:38.380 --> 01:10:41.540
out of these three
terms, where are we

01:10:41.540 --> 01:10:45.740
sure the linear part
is not coming from?

01:10:45.740 --> 01:10:49.180
So I guess you're probably
guessing this higher order terms

01:10:49.180 --> 01:10:50.540
element, right?

01:10:50.540 --> 01:10:52.860
So even just by
inspection, so we Taylor

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:58.880
expanded our increment, and
we are defining our variation,

01:10:58.880 --> 01:11:02.780
that is the linear part that
well approximates our increment,

01:11:02.780 --> 01:11:05.320
as this guy over here.

01:11:08.260 --> 01:11:11.380
Does that make sense?

01:11:11.380 --> 01:11:12.060
Yes?

01:11:12.060 --> 01:11:17.400
Does the term in
the parentheses,

01:11:17.400 --> 01:11:20.690
does that just represent all
the higher order terms networks

01:11:20.690 --> 01:11:22.080
that you're not considering?

01:11:22.080 --> 01:11:24.380
Yeah, exactly.

01:11:24.380 --> 01:11:25.360
OK.

01:11:25.360 --> 01:11:32.020
Now, if we look back at the
fundamental theorem of calculus

01:11:32.020 --> 01:11:34.020
of variation, which is
what we want to apply,

01:11:34.020 --> 01:11:35.980
we are seeing that the
condition that we had

01:11:35.980 --> 01:11:39.500
is basically that the
variation of x delta x

01:11:39.500 --> 01:11:42.100
needs to be equal to 0.

01:11:42.100 --> 01:11:46.220
Now, what we got here
is the variation.

01:11:46.220 --> 01:11:50.260
But this is also a
function of x dot.

01:11:50.260 --> 01:11:51.860
So what we're
going to do here is

01:11:51.860 --> 01:11:54.900
we're going to massage this
term in order to have everything

01:11:54.900 --> 01:11:59.020
as a function of delta x.

01:11:59.020 --> 01:11:59.543
Yes?

01:11:59.543 --> 01:12:00.043
All right.

01:12:00.043 --> 01:12:03.580
Could you just quickly say
how you got capital delta J

01:12:03.580 --> 01:12:05.680
to small delta j?

01:12:05.680 --> 01:12:06.220
Yeah.

01:12:06.220 --> 01:12:13.820
So let's see if
it's somewhere here.

01:12:13.820 --> 01:12:17.180
So if you remember, the way
we did it kind of abstractly

01:12:17.180 --> 01:12:21.420
is that we defined capital
delta J, so the increment,

01:12:21.420 --> 01:12:25.540
as some linear part
plus some stuff.

01:12:25.540 --> 01:12:30.460
What we're doing here is we
want to derive the linear part

01:12:30.460 --> 01:12:32.260
for this specific case.

01:12:32.260 --> 01:12:36.740
So we started by just applying
the definition of increment

01:12:36.740 --> 01:12:40.080
to our specific functional,
which led us to this.

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:48.220
Well, actually, it led
us to this guy over here.

01:12:48.220 --> 01:12:50.360
Then we wanted to
extract the linear part.

01:12:50.360 --> 01:12:52.900
And so we use Taylor
expansion of this first term

01:12:52.900 --> 01:12:57.605
to get the linear part,
which led us to this.

01:12:57.605 --> 01:12:58.980
Here, the first
and the last term

01:12:58.980 --> 01:13:02.940
cancel out and out
of these three terms,

01:13:02.940 --> 01:13:05.040
here, we have this
higher order terms,

01:13:05.040 --> 01:13:08.060
which is definitely not the
linear part we are looking for.

01:13:08.060 --> 01:13:11.460
And so we're just left
with the middle two.

01:13:11.460 --> 01:13:13.020
Yeah.

01:13:13.020 --> 01:13:13.520
OK.

01:13:13.520 --> 01:13:14.980
And now, we said
we want to express

01:13:14.980 --> 01:13:18.140
this only as a function of
delta x not delta x dot.

01:13:18.140 --> 01:13:21.220
And so we want to
massage this second term.

01:13:21.220 --> 01:13:24.680
The way we do this is
through integration by parts.

01:13:34.060 --> 01:13:35.640
So remember that--

01:13:41.540 --> 01:13:43.280
I think we lost that print.

01:13:47.985 --> 01:13:52.220
So remember that we--

01:13:52.220 --> 01:13:56.720
integration by parts
essentially entails doing this.

01:13:56.720 --> 01:14:02.460
So the integral of udv
is equal to u times

01:14:02.460 --> 01:14:05.660
v minus the integral of vdu.

01:14:10.620 --> 01:14:12.460
And now, we want
to basically apply

01:14:12.460 --> 01:14:14.840
this in the context
of our second term.

01:14:14.840 --> 01:14:22.820
So what we have is this one here
from integral from t0 to tf of g

01:14:22.820 --> 01:14:31.860
of x dot x dot t
delta x dot in the t.

01:14:31.860 --> 01:14:37.700
So we want to apply integration
by parts on this one here,

01:14:37.700 --> 01:14:44.480
where we're considering
this as u and this as dv.

01:14:47.660 --> 01:14:55.620
And this essentially means,
well, applying the integration

01:14:55.620 --> 01:14:56.500
by parts.

01:14:56.500 --> 01:15:13.100
So we have g of x dot x, x dot
t delta x dot delta x t0, tf.

01:15:13.100 --> 01:15:19.060
So this is the
first part minus--

01:15:19.060 --> 01:15:20.700
and I'm not sure if
you're able to see,

01:15:20.700 --> 01:15:24.240
but unfortunately, I'm
out of dark markers now.

01:15:28.220 --> 01:15:34.500
So the integral between
t0 and tf, right?

01:15:34.500 --> 01:15:35.360
Let's try again.

01:15:43.940 --> 01:15:45.180
This is great.

01:15:45.180 --> 01:15:53.820
So the integral between
t0 and tf of delta

01:15:53.820 --> 01:16:03.040
x times the derivative with
respect to time of g x dot x,

01:16:03.040 --> 01:16:16.460
x dot t entity,
which essentially

01:16:16.460 --> 01:16:24.820
means that now if we take this
and we write the full variation

01:16:24.820 --> 01:16:28.820
delta j, what we're getting
is the integral with respect--

01:16:28.820 --> 01:16:36.220
between t0 and tf
of g of x, x dot

01:16:36.220 --> 01:16:39.900
t minus the derivative
with respect

01:16:39.900 --> 01:17:00.900
to time of g x dot x plus
the gx dot t also computed

01:17:00.900 --> 01:17:02.220
between t0 and tf.

01:17:04.750 --> 01:17:07.710
So here, we use
integration by parts

01:17:07.710 --> 01:17:10.850
to massage the second term here.

01:17:10.850 --> 01:17:17.070
And then we just rewrote
the full variation delta j.

01:17:17.070 --> 01:17:23.790
Now, if we look a little
bit closer at the last term

01:17:23.790 --> 01:17:24.530
over here--

01:17:27.790 --> 01:17:34.130
actually, I think I
forgot delta x here.

01:17:39.890 --> 01:17:40.390
Yes.

01:17:46.590 --> 01:17:49.590
So if we look at
this term over here,

01:17:49.590 --> 01:17:53.770
we can safely say that
all of this is equal to 0.

01:17:56.470 --> 01:18:00.430
Because at t0 and
tf the variations

01:18:00.430 --> 01:18:04.070
that are admissible in
our x are actually none.

01:18:04.070 --> 01:18:08.910
So we said that x at t0
and x at tf are fixed.

01:18:08.910 --> 01:18:14.270
And so the variations that are
admissible are basically none.

01:18:14.270 --> 01:18:19.510
Again, visually, what we're
saying is that in our problem

01:18:19.510 --> 01:18:29.630
we have fixed x of tf
and x0 at t0 and tf.

01:18:29.630 --> 01:18:32.390
And so this means that we
have some fixed boundary

01:18:32.390 --> 01:18:33.830
conditions for x.

01:18:33.830 --> 01:18:36.190
And so the admissible
delta x here

01:18:36.190 --> 01:18:38.870
are none because all of my
points need to pass there.

01:18:38.870 --> 01:18:42.070
And so if these are all
zeros, then this entire term

01:18:42.070 --> 01:18:43.510
is equal to 0.

01:18:43.510 --> 01:18:46.850
And so we're left with
this term over here.

01:18:50.110 --> 01:18:51.450
OK.

01:18:55.150 --> 01:18:58.910
So for us, we're basically left
that the fundamental theorem

01:18:58.910 --> 01:19:01.230
for calculus of
variation results

01:19:01.230 --> 01:19:04.590
in this integral over here.

01:19:04.590 --> 01:19:14.230
It's your tf of g of x, x
of t minus the derivative

01:19:14.230 --> 01:19:17.150
with respect to
time of g of x dot,

01:19:17.150 --> 01:19:24.890
x dot times dx dt equal to 0.

01:19:24.890 --> 01:19:28.190
So our condition for fundamental
calculus of variation

01:19:28.190 --> 01:19:30.470
is that this guy is equal to 0.

01:19:30.470 --> 01:19:35.830
And so here, we are just
setting delta j equal to 0.

01:19:35.830 --> 01:19:37.990
Yes?

01:19:37.990 --> 01:19:40.670
Is the admissible dx
that you're talking about

01:19:40.670 --> 01:19:47.510
captured in that dx inside the
bracket, or is it [INAUDIBLE]?

01:19:47.510 --> 01:19:48.647
It's in the dx.

01:19:48.647 --> 01:19:49.230
This one here.

01:19:49.230 --> 01:19:52.390
Is that admissible [INAUDIBLE]?

01:19:52.390 --> 01:19:57.070
I mean, in general, we are
looking at the variations

01:19:57.070 --> 01:19:59.630
of our functions dx.

01:19:59.630 --> 01:20:02.830
And for the problem
that we're looking at,

01:20:02.830 --> 01:20:06.790
the admissible variations
of x at the boundary points

01:20:06.790 --> 01:20:08.410
are always 0.

01:20:14.790 --> 01:20:15.750
OK.

01:20:15.750 --> 01:20:16.650
That is [INAUDIBLE].

01:20:16.650 --> 01:20:17.150
OK.

01:20:17.150 --> 01:20:17.650
Yeah.

01:20:17.650 --> 01:20:19.310
And so I just want
to wrap this up,

01:20:19.310 --> 01:20:22.173
so I don't steal too
much of your time.

01:20:22.173 --> 01:20:23.590
But essentially,
what we're saying

01:20:23.590 --> 01:20:27.150
is that for us the fundamental
theorem of calculus of variation

01:20:27.150 --> 01:20:32.990
leads to exactly that condition
over there, which is the one I'm

01:20:32.990 --> 01:20:35.990
written here on the slide.

01:20:35.990 --> 01:20:40.190
Now, does this tell us
anything about the inner--

01:20:40.190 --> 01:20:44.390
like the integrand
term over here?

01:20:44.390 --> 01:20:45.210
It does.

01:20:45.210 --> 01:20:47.470
And it does so through
the last result

01:20:47.470 --> 01:20:49.070
that I'm introducing
today, which

01:20:49.070 --> 01:20:52.510
is the fundamental lemma of
calculus of variation, which

01:20:52.510 --> 01:20:57.230
essentially says that for
any continuous function h,

01:20:57.230 --> 01:21:00.590
so any given continuous function
h, the integral between t0

01:21:00.590 --> 01:21:06.510
and tf of h of t times
delta x, so if this is true,

01:21:06.510 --> 01:21:08.550
so if we have this
result here, this

01:21:08.550 --> 01:21:13.990
is only true if
h is 0 everywhere

01:21:13.990 --> 01:21:18.030
in the interval t0 tf.

01:21:18.030 --> 01:21:20.910
What is the intuition
here is that--

01:21:20.910 --> 01:21:29.750
say that this is our h of t
and these are the possible

01:21:29.750 --> 01:21:36.310
variations delta x of t given--
if we have any variable function

01:21:36.310 --> 01:21:39.670
h of t, say one that is none--

01:21:39.670 --> 01:21:42.710
is positive at
some time t, if we

01:21:42.710 --> 01:21:46.390
were to pick our variations
adversarially, our delta x of t,

01:21:46.390 --> 01:21:48.350
we could always pick
the point that kind of

01:21:48.350 --> 01:21:54.830
matches the variation in x in
order to make this positive.

01:21:54.830 --> 01:21:57.950
And so the only way in
which this entire integral

01:21:57.950 --> 01:22:05.110
is equal to 0 is if h
of t is 0 everywhere.

01:22:05.110 --> 01:22:07.830
Does that make sense?

01:22:07.830 --> 01:22:10.630
So through this, we essentially
are finished in the sense

01:22:10.630 --> 01:22:15.670
that we arrived at
our condition here.

01:22:15.670 --> 01:22:18.690
And then we said that
through the lemma,

01:22:18.690 --> 01:22:24.270
we know that this essentially
means that this would be our h.

01:22:24.270 --> 01:22:26.430
And this is delta x.

01:22:26.430 --> 01:22:28.310
All of this integral
is equal to 0

01:22:28.310 --> 01:22:31.470
only if this part
here is equal to 0.

01:22:31.470 --> 01:22:33.870
And so here, we have
fast tracked Newton.

01:22:33.870 --> 01:22:35.730
He basically took 20 years.

01:22:35.730 --> 01:22:38.990
We took 45 minutes to
reach to the same result.

01:22:38.990 --> 01:22:41.723
And we arrived to the
necessary optimality condition

01:22:41.723 --> 01:22:43.390
for the infinite
dimensional case, which

01:22:43.390 --> 01:22:45.670
is known as the Euler equation.

01:22:45.670 --> 01:22:47.450
I'm out of time, so I
won't stick further,

01:22:47.450 --> 01:22:50.030
but I'll recap
this on Wednesday.

01:22:50.030 --> 01:22:51.860
Thank you.

